For your COCO-style collection (about 123,287 images with five captions each), the key recommendation was to keep image records separate from caption records, linked by an image ID, and index that relationship. For example:

```sql
CREATE TABLE images (
    id BIGINT PRIMARY KEY,
    url TEXT NOT NULL
);

CREATE TABLE captions (
    id BIGINT PRIMARY KEY,
    image_id BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES images(id),
    text TEXT NOT NULL
);

CREATE INDEX captions_image_id_idx ON captions(image_id);
```

That lets you retrieve an image with its associated captions efficiently, while preserving the one-to-many relationship. For training, use a PyTorch `Dataset` to load and preprocess each image and its captions on demand, then use a `DataLoader` to batch and shuffle examples rather than loading the entire collection into memory. For evaluation, keep separate train, validation, and test splits so metrics are measured on images not used for training.

